9725191630423,-0.05542561039328575,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-0.0015115038258954883,0.0,-1.3112935448589269e-05,-2.8013790142722428e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-4.768370445162873e-07,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-1.1920922133867862e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,48336,594,15198,572,220,17,15,15,2849,4134,13,11201,374,7920,13,1913,1128,1899,315,279,2003,1521,806,15198,4399,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nKim's birthday was 200 days ago. Today is Wednesday. On what day of the week did his birthday fall?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e207a4fbd1c51b48b4091b86909cfea833eac447d0fc7df8a56d1a4118f7633d"},"seconds":6.090943813323975,"seed":999558515455183190,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the day of the week for Kim's birthday, we need to work backwards from today, which is Wednesday. We need to consider how the days of the week cycle every 7 days and then use the information that 200 days have passed.\n\nFirst, we find the remainder when 200 is divided by 7, because the days of the week repeat every 7 days. This can be done using the modulo operation:\n\n\\[ 200 \\div 7 = 28 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n\nThis means that 200 days is equivalent to 28 weeks and 4 extra days. Since 28 weeks is a whole number of weeks, it brings us back to the same day of the week. So, we only need to count forward 4 days from today (Wednesday).\n\nLet's count forward 4 days from Wednesday:\n- 1 day after Wednesday is Thursday\n- 2 days after Wednesday is Friday\n- 3 days after Wednesday is Saturday\n- 4 days after Wednesday is Sunday\n\nTherefore, Kim's birthday was on a Sunday. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Sunday}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\text{Sunday}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:49"}
